+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I get a CLUE report on a house I want to buy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Only the owner, an insurer or a lender can order the CLUE report on a house. A buyer asks the seller to order it, and we make that request before you offer. It covers only recent years."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I see FEMA flood claims by address?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not as a buyer. Federal privacy law bars FEMA from giving out a house's flood claims without the owner's consent. Once you own the house, you can ask FEMA for its claims, including those from before you owned it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Zone X mean the house never flooded?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. It means the house is outside FEMA's high-risk flood zone, and nearly 1 in 4 paid flood claims in Horry County were on buildings rated outside that zone. In Florence it was 45 percent."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a severe repetitive loss property?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"FEMA gives the name to a house with four or more paid flood claims over $5,000 each. A house with two or more building payments that add up to more than its value counts too. At least two of the payments must be within 10 years of each other, counting since 1978. The house pays a 15 percent surcharge on federal flood insurance, or the prior-claims charge if that is higher."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will the lender tell me if the house is in a flood zone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, if the house is in FEMA's high-risk zone and the lender is a regulated lender. The lender must make sure you are told in writing a reasonable time before you sign. The notice is about the zone, not about past floods."}}]}</script>
